  /* Only a card that HAS a url gets a clickable surface, and it gets it from a
     real anchor, so it is keyboard reachable and takes the focus ring with it.
     The card itself carried `cursor: pointer` on all seventeen. */
  /* The picture box. It was 160px tall with a scrim over its FULL height
     (`from-black/70 via-black/16 to-transparent`), so the lower half of every
     screenshot was dimmed whether anything sat on it or not: on the
     QualityCluster shot the picture read as fog.
     The label was NOT a contrast failure, and it is worth writing down that it
     was measured rather than assumed. On the live page the ground under it ran
     5.27:1 (Django SLA) to 10.89:1 against white, all of it past AA. What hurt
     was the ground being BUSY, not dark enough: on a shot of a text page the
     site copy ran straight under the glyphs. So the fix is a foot band tall
     enough for the label and nothing more, blurred behind (see the scrim), which
     leaves the picture above it untouched. Measured again after: 6.69:1 to
     19.62:1 across all seventeen cards, identical in both themes.
     The band is mixed from the dark ground (#020617) rather than pure black so
     it sits in the page instead of punching a hole in it, and the label keeps
     its own shadow for wherever the band runs thin. The box grew to 192px
     because a screenshot cropped to 160px inside a 438px-wide card is a sliver;
     paired with `object-top`, a dashboard now shows its head instead of a
     random middle slice. */
  /* A RATIO, not a frozen height. The 192px above were tuned for a 438px card,
     and 438/192 is 2.28, which is exactly the ratio the twelve illustrations are
     drawn at (1600x702). So the house ratio was already the right answer, it was
     just written down as one number that only holds at one width.

     What the frozen height did everywhere else, measured on 2026-09-04: at a
     viewport of 767px the card is 711px wide, the box stays 192px, the ratio
     becomes 3.70 and the Quality-Dashboard shot loses 56.7 percent of its height.
     At 1024px the card is 303px, the ratio drops to 1.58 and the Reise-App shot
     loses 30.9 percent of its WIDTH instead. The crop wandered with the window,
     so a visitor on a laptop and one on a tablet saw different parts of the same
     evidence.

     With the ratio, twelve of the seventeen pictures show whole at every width,
     and the five real screenshots crop the same way on every screen. */
  .cv-shot { aspect-ratio: 1600 / 702; }

  .cv-shot-scrim {
    position: absolute;
    inset: auto 0 0 0;
    /* Was a flat 4.5rem, which was fine while the box was always 192px. Now the
       box follows the card width, and on a narrow desktop column (303px card,
       133px box) a fixed 72px band would cover 54 percent of the picture. The
       cap keeps the band from eating the evidence it sits on. */
    height: min(4.5rem, 45%);
    background: linear-gradient(
      to top,
      rgba(2, 6, 23, 0.82),
      rgba(2, 6, 23, 0.44) 55%,
      rgba(2, 6, 23, 0)
    );
    /* The band alone is not enough where the picture under it is a page of body
       copy: on the QualityCluster shot the site text read straight through it and
       fought the label glyph for glyph. The blur turns whatever is under the band
       into a wash, so the label always sits on an even ground. It costs nothing on
       the illustrations, whose lower edge is flat colour anyway. */
    /* Unprefixed only. Writing the -webkit- twin by hand made the build emit the
       PREFIXED declaration and drop the standard one, so the browser computed
       `backdrop-filter: none` while the source looked correct. Caught by reading
       the shipped stylesheet and the computed style, not the source. The bundler
       adds whatever prefix its targets need. */
    backdrop-filter: blur(4px);
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .cv-shot-category {
    color: #fff;
    text-shadow: 0 1px 3px rgba(2, 6, 23, 0.92);
  }

  /* The overlay PAINTS the focus ring across the card and takes no clicks.
     It used to take them: `inset: 0` on an <a> spreads a live hit area over the
     whole card, so the blue category line, the tech chips and the body copy all
     opened the card's url, and none of the three is a link. Measured at five
     points on the agent-mesh card, every one of them resolved to the same
     anchor. `pointer-events: none` keeps the ring, drops the hit area, and
     gives text selection back. The title stays the click target it looks like. */
  .cv-project-link::after {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .cv-project-link:focus-visible::after {
    outline: 2px solid var(--accent-hover);
